If you're thinking of trading in your current iPhone for this year's latest, consider the following features rumored to be coming to next September's iPhone 20 models (Apple is expected to skip the iPhone 19 for its commemorative edition). Taken together, they may just give you pause for thought.<br/>
<ul><br/>
  <li><strong>A quad-curved, all-glass design:</strong> The display glass <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/05/12/20th-anniversary-iphone-all-screen-design/">reportedly</a> curves away on all four sides of the iPhone 20, concealing the metal frame during normal use for a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/05/14/20th-anniversary-iphone-no-visible-frame-bezels/">borderless effect</a>. Apple is said to be working with Samsung on an "equal-depth quad-curved" display to achieve the feat.</li><br/>
<li><strong>Advanced OLED technology:</strong> Apple is planning to use a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/09/23/20th-anniversary-iphone-brighter-oled/">brighter, thinner OLED panel</a> for the 20th anniversary iPhone. Rumors suggest that Apple will adopt Samsung ‌OLED‌ displays with Color Filter on Encapsulation (COE) technology, which reduces the thickness of the overall display stack, and lets more light through to improve brightness while reducing power draw.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>Under-display Face ID and camera:</strong> Apple is reportedly continuing to test hardware that would eliminate the Dynamic Island – the visible cutout – from the front of the device, with Face ID components <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/10/22/three-new-iphone-designs-allegedly-revealed/">moving under the screen</a> and an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/26/apple-20th-anniversary-iphone-all-screen/">under-camera display</a> said to be in development. Whether the technology will be ready in time for a fall 2027 launch remains <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/01/22/20th-anniversary-iphone-all-screen-design-in-doubt/">uncertain</a>.</li><br/>
<li><strong>Custom Apple image sensor:</strong> Apple is said to be developing its own <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/10/27/apple-custom-image-sensor-20th-anniversary-iphone/">stacked image sensor</a> to replace the Sony sensors it has traditionally used for the iPhone. Apple has reportedly already developed a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/08/04/apple-developing-its-own-image-sensor/">working prototype</a>. The sensor is said to use LOFIC technology, which would let each pixel store varying amounts of light and potentially reach up to 20 stops of dynamic range.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>Solid-state buttons:</strong> Apple is said to be testing <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/10/28/20th-anniversary-iphone-solid-state-buttons/">haptic replacements</a> for the Side, Volume, Action, and Camera Control buttons. The buttons could be integrated directly into the device's frame, with no actual physical movement when pressed, yet retaining a tactile responsiveness.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>A 2-nanometer A21 chip:</strong> The 20th anniversary models are expected to feature a second-generation 2-nanometer chip. With the upcoming iPhone 18 Pro models widely expected to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/29/leaked-a20-pro-image-iphone-18-pro-performance/">debut the A20 Pro chip</a>, the anniversary models would likely be powered by the A21 Pro.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>Silicon anode battery:</strong> Apple is reportedly exploring a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/05/14/2027-iphone-game-changing-silicon-battery/">new battery technology</a> that uses a silicon anode capable of storing more lithium ions per gram, potentially delivering longer battery life without increasing the physical size of the battery.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>Mobile high bandwidth memory:</strong> Apple is rumored to be evaluating <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/05/14/2027-iphones-advanced-ai-memory-tech/">vertically stacked DRAM technology</a> to improve memory bandwidth and boost on-device AI performance.</li><br/>
  <li><strong>Two sizes:</strong> The two anniversary models could have a larger display size compared to this September's iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone 18 Pro Max, which are expected to use 6.3-inch and 6.9-inch displays similar to iPhone 17 Pro models. In contrast, Apple is rumored to be <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/04/bigger-displays-rumored-for-next-year-iphones/">prototyping</a> a 6.4-inch display for the iPhone 20 Pro, and a 7-inch display for the larger 20 Pro Max.<br/>
</li><br/>
</ul>With all that said, we're still 12 months out, and Apple's plans shift constantly behind closed doors, plus features this ambitious have a habit of slipping a year or two. Under-display Face ID in particular has been "two years away" for about five years now.<br/>
